WinZip AES-192 encryption in libarchive's zip writer.

One of this project's patches to libarchive, which every build (macOS, Linux and
Windows) applies with patch_libarchive (native/build-config.sh), to the libarchive
release pinned there, in the order that LIBARCHIVE_PATCHES there lists them. A build
fails if any hunk does not apply exactly (no fuzz), and scripts/check-native-updates.sh
will not propose a libarchive release that it does not apply to. See "The libarchive
patches" in native/BUILD.md.

libarchive's zip writer encrypts with WinZip AES-128 (encryption=aes128) and AES-256
(encryption=aes256), but not AES-192, although its zip reader decrypts all three
(strengths 1, 2 and 3 in init_WinZip_AES_decryption()). The crypto backends that the
builds use take a 24-byte AES key as they take a 16- or a 32-byte one: CommonCrypto
on macOS, CNG (BCrypt) on Windows and mbedTLS on Linux. Their PBKDF2 and HMAC-SHA1
take any length.

This patch adds encryption=aes192, which writes WinZip AES-192 as the zip reader
reads it: strength 2 in the AES extra field (0x9901), a 12-byte salt, and a 24-byte
AES key and a 24-byte HMAC-SHA1 key, which PBKDF2 derives from the passphrase as for
the other key sizes. The 2-byte password verification value and the 10-byte
authentication code are as before, and so are AES-128 and AES-256. In the zip writer:

1. The encryption option accepts aes192 if the crypto backend can encrypt with it,
   as it accepts aes128 and aes256 (is_winzip_aes_encryption_supported()).
2. ENCRYPTION_WINZIP_AES192 and WINZIP_AES192_HEADER_SIZE (12 + 2) are added beside
   the AES-128 and AES-256 ones, and ENCRYPTION_WINZIP_AES192 is handled wherever
   those are: such an entry is encrypted, has compression method 99 and the AES
   extra field in its local and central directory headers, and the size of a stored
   one allows for its salt, password verification value and authentication code.
3. The AES extra field gives strength 2 for it.
4. init_winzip_aes_encryption() and is_winzip_aes_encryption_supported() take a
   12-byte salt and a 24-byte key for it.

libarchive's manual pages, which list the values of the encryption option
(archive_write_set_options.3 and bsdtar.1), and its tests are not changed: this
project ships neither.

The new encryption value is marked LIBARCHIVE_NET_ZIP_AES192, the marker that
LIBARCHIVE_PATCHES gives this patch: patch_libarchive checks that every file the
patch changes has it. Every other change names ENCRYPTION_WINZIP_AES192 or
WINZIP_AES192_HEADER_SIZE.

--- a/libarchive/archive_write_set_format_zip.c
+++ b/libarchive/archive_write_set_format_zip.c
@@ -108,6 +108,11 @@
 	ENCRYPTION_NONE	= 0,
 	ENCRYPTION_TRADITIONAL, /* Traditional PKWARE encryption. */
 	ENCRYPTION_WINZIP_AES128, /* WinZIP AES-128 encryption. */
+	/*
+	 * LIBARCHIVE_NET_ZIP_AES192: WinZip AES-192 (strength 2), which the
+	 * zip reader already decrypts, can be written as encryption=aes192.
+	 */
+	ENCRYPTION_WINZIP_AES192, /* WinZIP AES-192 encryption. */
 	ENCRYPTION_WINZIP_AES256, /* WinZIP AES-256 encryption. */
 };
 
@@ -121,6 +126,7 @@
